{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread","id":306933,"title":"Religion Thread","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-11-08T14:54:59-08:00","id":306933,"title":"Religion Thread","content":"Thread for people to talk about any and all religious issues. No trolling, insulting other's beliefs or harassment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1410-czernobog","name":"Czernobog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-08T15:20:12-08:00","id":306953,"title":"","content":"Well, I know from experience (and research) that the first post is often key in setting the tone of these things, so I'll open with a positive one.<br /> <br /> My relationship with religion is...complicated. I don't really want to get into the personal side, so I'll talk about my mental/academic perspective. I enjoy studying religions on a lot of bases: they're new ideas, they tell you a lot about the cultures they're practised in, and there aren't a lot of religions I've studied that I haven't found worthwhile ideas in. Christianity supports the idea of redemption and being kind to one another, Buddhism is about letting go of attachments, Vodoun very much embodies acceptance, and the like. Are some of these ideas mutually exclusive? Sometimes, but often I find it's more a matter of context.<br /> <br /> Most religious people I've known have been pretty cool too, and those that weren't I'm pretty sure would have been tools regardless.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-08T19:42:14-08:00","id":307101,"title":"","content":"Oh boy, new show.<br /> <br /> Hopefully this'll be another one that just putters out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T02:30:01-08:00","id":307278,"title":"","content":"I'm atheist, but culturally Abrahamic.<br /> <br /> ^I will wonder when it gets ultimately derailed while watching from the sidelines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T05:41:47-08:00","id":307342,"title":"","content":"I, personally, believe in the soul more than I believe in God - which is to say, the only thing I really believe in is the persistence of consciousness and self. I do believe to a lesser degree, that the divine is manifest in the structure of the Cosmos itself.<br /> These ideas are the foundation of my own spiritual beliefs.<br /> <br /> I study other religions, however. I like to learn about other systems of belief and what makes people believe in them. Hell, my religious studies class was, for a long time, the absolute centre of my social life. I'm thinking I might go further into the study of such things eventually.<br /> I'm deeply interested in this sort of stuff, so I hope this thread can be home to some cordial discussion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Raksi plays Peek-a-boo for keeps. ~ nalak42","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/203-kleptomania","name":"Kleptomania","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar203_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T05:46:58-08:00","id":307344,"title":"","content":"Same here (EDIT: Kleptomania's post did not exist when I began typing mine, so I'm referring to Kat's here). Raised nondemoninationally Christian, became an atheist because I was unable to find either anything particular to believe in or any reluctance not to believe in anything once longstanding habit was taken out of the issue. I do my best not to be one of those prick atheists who rags on others for their faith, and I think I do a good job of it because I genuinely don't want to take anyone's faith from them, but every now and then I slip up when I hear about someone using God as an excuse to be an asshole to people (during discussions of gay rights, more often than not).<br /> <br /> I guess that paragraph is a long way of saying I don't expect to have much to contribute to this thread.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":271,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","user":"JimB","date":". 11-09-2014, 09:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T05:52:57-08:00","id":307349,"title":"","content":"I had Jews, Muslims, Catholic and Orthodox Christians (and a Buddhist) in my family line, I think that along with my cousin I'm first generation open atheist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T06:56:16-08:00","id":307379,"title":"","content":"Hmf...I was born Catholic, and was an altar boy for a good 5-6 years (unmolested thank you) even thought of becoming a priest. (I was 12 years old at the time) Things changed when I was a teenager. I took a more atheistic slant when I got even older but that later didn't make logical sense to me. To my mind pure objectivity demands &quot;possibility,&quot; potential, a middle ground. To deny an existence of anything would demand knowledge of what can and cannot exist. That's an arrogance I couldn't accept. Even worse when I added that humanity is barely self-aware...<br /> And I firmly believe in spirituality, it just seems a matter of fact to me that the soul exists.<br /> So I see a 3d axis of neutrality that I'm in the middle of; Agnostic-Gnostic; and Atheist-Theist. And I'm sitting rather comfortably in the middle exploring beliefs here and there. Seeing how beliefs evolve...<br /> I recall an image made in homage to Carl Jung that I find appropriate.<br /> From <a href=\"http://www.theatlantic.com/health/archive/2012/03/memories-dreams-reflections-a-rare-glimpse-into-carl-jungs-mind/254513/\" target=\"_blank\">Memories, Dreams, Reflections</a>, by Carl Jung: (spoilred for size)<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><img src=\"http://www.brainpickings.org/wp-content/uploads/2012/03/carljungaustinkleon.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T07:29:12-08:00","id":307397,"title":"","content":"Eh, I would refrain from such a joke in this thread like one in your first sentence.<br /> <br /> BTW, my high school chaplain was a cool guy but got relocated to Spain.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T08:55:43-08:00","id":307457,"title":"","content":"The monsignor in my church was convicted later of being a pedophile, so it wasn't much a joke. We were just lucky enough that he didn't do any that sort of thing in that particular church.<br /> Regardless, I think of my childhood Catholicism as a positive experience. I sometime wish I could feel that way again today.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T09:47:18-08:00","id":307525,"title":"","content":"I was born into a Catholic home like most people in latin countries, and thought of myself a believer until I was twelve. I can still remember the day my doubts started, it was by the end of a Science class in the 7th grade at 12 years old, I was saying something about God when a classmate said &quot;Are you stupid or what? Haven't you been studying? Evolution got us where we are, not God.&quot;. And that sentence stuck with me and totally transformed. I started questioning everything and with the years a I grew into disbelief.<br /> <br /> The problem with religion is they forfeit logic and rationality in favour of Dogma. And I can't abide by that. If you want me to believe in something, show me it is real, give me a logical explanation for whatever you can't show. The more I questioned the less answers I got.<br /> <br /> Ironically I love to study religion. I know various and it's funny because most times I know them better than the believers. That's another thing I can't understand, believers don't care about what they believe. Most people follow their family/country religion blindly and have no idea about it's precepts, origins, beliefs, history, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2435-prophet","name":"Prophet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2435_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T10:10:39-08:00","id":307555,"title":"","content":"I'm a Christian atheist. Really hard to explain the concept in a few lines that will be satisfactory, but the basic gist is that I believe God is dead existentially and cosmic-historically.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thinking as I do that the Creator of this World is a very Cruel Being &amp; being a Worshipper of Christ, I cannot help saying: &quot;the Son, O how unlike the Father!&quot; First God Almighty comes with a Thump on the Head. Then Jesus Christ comes with a balm to heal it. - William Blake, Christian mystic and visionary","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/774-pilgrim","name":"Pilgrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar774_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-09T18:51:19-08:00","id":307726,"title":"","content":"Well, we do have our beliefs...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-10T00:16:25-08:00","id":307941,"title":"","content":"^^Is there any overlap between your stance and Nietzsche's philosophy?<br /> <br /> I think that even if a creator being existed, it would be of such magnitude it would not even notice Solar System.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":2918,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","user":"Kat","date":". 11-10-2014, 03:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-10T04:14:54-08:00","id":308035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pilgrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=307555#post307555\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a Christian atheist. Really hard to explain the concept in a few lines that will be satisfactory, but the basic gist is that I believe God is dead existentially and cosmic-historically.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I see you take after Prospero with his immortal words &quot;If a god of love and life ever did exist... he is long since dead. Someone... something, rules in his place.&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2435-prophet","name":"Prophet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2435_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-10T07:19:52-08:00","id":308182,"title":"","content":"I generally refer to myself as a &quot;agnostic with a strong atheistic leaning.&quot; I don't believe in any form of god or spirits, but can't rule out completely the existence of any spiritual power. But even if someone would be able to come up with scientifically sound proof of some sort of higher power, it still wouldn't change how I lived; so in a way it is moot.<br /> <br /> But I love learning about different religions and talk to people with a world view completely different from mine. I guess it is in part of me being a roleplayer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2839-lundgren","name":"Lundgren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-10T08:03:30-08:00","id":308218,"title":"","content":"Due to some recent issues, I've revised this from earlier, similar posts.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I am an Absurdist Taoist<br /> <br /> From the top, Absurdist.<br /> For those of you who don\u2019t know, Absurdism is the school of thought that comes from the conflict between people searching for meaning and never really finding anything solid. This line of thought, when pursued to the usual conclusions (we\u2019ll never know the meaning of life/there is no meaning of life), usually results in 3 reactions.<br /> 1 \u2013 If there is no meaning to life, then why bother living it (aka suicide), which I whole-heartedly disagree with.<br /> 2 \u2013 Philosophical suicide, clinging to an intangible and empirically improvable thing (aka most religious/spiritual beliefs) to create meaning.<br /> 3 \u2013 Demonic madness, which is acknowledging the absurdity and continuing to live in spite of it, which is simultaneously accepting of it and revolting against it.<br /> For myself, I find I waffle back and forth between 2 and 3. I attempt to build my own meaning, piece meal from various religious, spiritual and philosophical sources. I take what I like and discard the rest, since accepting whole-cloth any thoughts that I do not completely agree with seems absurd to me. Why force myself to fit into someone else\u2019s ideas? It\u2019s my mind, not theirs, and I consider this to work in both directions. I certainly wouldn\u2019t expect anyone else to try to fit into my mold.<br /> I also acknowledge that whatever meanings I do come up with for my life are ultimately meaningless to the universe as a whole. I will be here for just a few decades, which is nothing but a drop into the river of time. I will simply go with that flow, doing my best to avoid the rocks and to flow around them when I can\u2019t.<br /> For this reason, I am (at the core) an atheist. I do not believe that any gods, demons, or divinity is required. I don\u2019t need any of those things to tell me what is right or wrong, to give meaning to my life, to talk to when I need guidance. If I can\u2019t figure these things out on my own, then I\u2019m not well. I don\u2019t need to be told that killing people is bad. All I need to do is realize that I wouldn\u2019t like it done to me, so I shouldn\u2019t do it to someone else. Sadly, this line of thought is also what makes me very bad at getting ahead in business/work situations, as I am simply not cut-throat enough to do some of the things other people seem to have no problem doing to me. Oh well, I am who I am.<br /> <br /> Part 2, Taoist<br /> Again, a brief explanation for those who don\u2019t know, Taoism (for me the philosophical branch is the base of it all) is a pseudo-religion that describes a way of living which is harmony with the natural ebb and flow of life. It promotes living life well and comfortable, with effortless action and minimal interference with other\u2019s lives. Sort of. There\u2019s more to it than that, and there are whole books on the subject. Personally, I recommend The Tao of Pooh, since it describes the whole concept using characters from Winnie the Pooh. From Taoism, I take the ideas of how to be a proper Me. I have an Inner Nature and an Inner Voice and a Center Self, and this is who I am, who I should be, how I should be, and why I am who I am. I don\u2019t try to be anything else but Me because that\u2019s difficult, if not ultimately impossible. I see Taoism as the tool to make the meaning from Absurdism. Life has no meaning, so I make my own, and I make it to be as sweet or sour or bitter as I allow it to be. In Taoism, there is a lot of \u201cletting go\u201d, in the sense of trying to control things that are out of your control. The best way I know to describe this is from Tao of Pooh talking about the scene where Pooh, Piglet and Rabbit take Tigger out into the woods to lose him. And they succeed, and get lost themselves in the process. Rabbit, being quite the control freak, insists that he knows the way home, but he keeps leading Pooh &amp; Piglet in circles. Finally, Rabbit gets lost all by himself, leaving Pooh &amp; Piglet alone. After a while, Pooh gets up and tells Piglet it\u2019s time to go home. Piglet asks if Pooh knows the way, and Pooh responds that he doesn\u2019t, but he\u2019s hungry and he\u2019s got a dozen Hunny Pots at home calling to him, and Rabbit was juts being too loud for him to hear them before. Pooh proceeds to wander off into the forest, with Piglet quietly following, and they find their way out and home again.<br /> Moral of the story: Sometimes, you have to be quiet and just let it happen to get what you want. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-11T11:04:25-08:00","id":309323,"title":"","content":"I'm still Buddhist (Tibetan Tradition, schooled mainly in the Gelug School).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-13T00:40:27-08:00","id":310993,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Papa Bear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=308218#post308218\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>Part 2, Taoist</i><br /> <i>Again, a brief explanation for those who don\u2019t know, Taoism (for me the philosophical branch is the base of it all) is a pseudo-religion that describes a way of living which is harmony with the natural ebb and flow of life. It promotes living life well and comfortable, with effortless action and minimal interference with other\u2019s lives. Sort of. There\u2019s more to it than that, and there are whole books on the subject. Personally, I recommend The Tao of Pooh, since it describes the whole concept using characters from Winnie the Pooh. From Taoism, I take the ideas of how to be a proper Me. I have an Inner Nature and an Inner Voice and a Center Self, and this is who I am, who I should be, how I should be, and why I am who I am. I don\u2019t try to be anything else but Me because that\u2019s difficult, if not ultimately impossible. I see Taoism as the tool to make the meaning from Absurdism. Life has no meaning, so I make my own, and I make it to be as sweet or sour or bitter as I allow it to be. In Taoism, there is a lot of \u201cletting go\u201d, in the sense of trying to control things that are out of your control. The best way I know to describe this is from Tao of Pooh talking about the scene where Pooh, Piglet and Rabbit take Tigger out into the woods to lose him. And they succeed, and get lost themselves in the process. Rabbit, being quite the control freak, insists that he knows the way home, but he keeps leading Pooh &amp; Piglet in circles. Finally, Rabbit gets lost all by himself, leaving Pooh &amp; Piglet alone. After a while, Pooh gets up and tells Piglet it\u2019s time to go home. Piglet asks if Pooh knows the way, and Pooh responds that he doesn\u2019t, but he\u2019s hungry and he\u2019s got a dozen Hunny Pots at home calling to him, and Rabbit was juts being too loud for him to hear them before. Pooh proceeds to wander off into the forest, with Piglet quietly following, and they find their way out and home again.</i><br /> <i>Moral of the story: Sometimes, you have to be quiet and just let it happen to get what you want.</i><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I remember the first time I read Tao Te Ching. It was around the same time I was reading Buddhism, and I remember thinking it's a mix of Terry Pratchett, 1984, and Buddhism.<br /> <br /> (Yes, I know the influence was most likely the other way around with Buddhism.)<br /> <br /> (And by Terry Pratchett I mean a lot of it struck me as being genre savvy in a broadly similar way as the book he keeps writing. Not in an insulting way, I assure you.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":207,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","user":"Allan53","date":". 11-13-2014, 03:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-17T09:50:48-08:00","id":314639,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=310993#post310993\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I remember the first time I read Tao Te Ching. It was around the same time I was reading Buddhism, and I remember thinking it's a mix of Terry Pratchett, 1984, and Buddhism.<br /> <br /> (Yes, I know the influence was most likely the other way around with Buddhism.)<br /> <br /> (And by Terry Pratchett I mean a lot of it struck me as being genre savvy in a broadly similar way as the book he keeps writing. Not in an insulting way, I assure you.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, you're not far off.<br /> <br /> Someone once said that Zen Buddhism is what happened when Taoism and Buddhism got together to compare notes.<br /> <br /> And while I'm not familiar enough with the Terry Pratchett books enough to fully get the reference (yeah, I know, I should be!), Taoism does seem to have been put together after watching Hinduism, Buddhism and Confucianism flounder around for a while, then decide how they suck and avoid those things. Keeps the whole thing very open ended.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-18T08:32:52-08:00","id":315421,"title":"","content":"I am Catholic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1090-tobias","name":"Tobias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1090_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-19T00:00:24-08:00","id":315967,"title":"","content":"The talk of Absurdism reminds me of an amusing little video I found about Camus done in an 8-bit aesthetic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://youtu.be/KI25easQs2k\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://youtu.be/KI25easQs2k\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://youtu.be/KI25easQs2k\" target=\"_blank\"> http://youtu.be/KI25easQs2k </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Personally, I haven't done much to investigate philosophy or the various schools of thought to decide which, if any of them, fits me best. A far as meaning goes, I kind of think of it like a human heuristic, not something that the universe can have independent of us... at least until the universe decides to start sharing its own thoughts on the matter, something I don't ever expect it to do.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-19T02:50:33-08:00","id":316099,"title":"","content":"Atheist here.<br /> I don't have any proof of the existance of the divine, and it's just like saying it doesn't. I respect the fact that other people prefer to believe, but atheism gave the strength to be alive. You only live once. Life has no inherent meaning, and you are the one who will give meaning to your life. Before becoming an atheist I've had suicidal thoughts, and I feared death.<br /> Now, while I don't welcome it, I'm fine with the fact that I'll die and disappear. This means that I have a really short time to be kind to other people, to make of my life what I really want and to be myself, and suicide is not a great way to do that.<br /> Even when I was a christian, I always felt that religion isn't what gives you morality. I've had no problem with the idea that other people who were not Christian could be moral.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/316-jesuncolo","name":"Jesuncolo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar316_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-19T14:38:22-08:00","id":316536,"title":"","content":"I've no proof of what happens after I die. And that terrifies me. To say &quot;I'll die and disappear&quot; is a fact, is more belief than anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-19T19:42:35-08:00","id":316880,"title":"","content":"I'm raised in a protestant Christian culture, but when asked about my religious views, I say I'm agnostic. That's kind of the most neutral you can be, and I'm as neutral to religions as the influences of the society I grew up in allow me to be. I don't mind the idea that there might be an afterlife, I'd quite like that, but I also don't let that interfere with my life on Earth. The plurality of religions, schisms and sects in the world tells me that there is no consensus. We do not have any trusted information to base an informed opinion about the divine on, and so, I will say nothing about it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Dex Davican wrote:</b> I can say without exaggeration or dishonesty that I am the most creative man <i>ever to have lived</i>","edited":{"userid":75,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/75-weimann","user":"Weimann","date":". 11-20-2014, 10:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/75-weimann","name":"Weimann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar75_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1279"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-11-20T01:05:49-08:00","id":317099,"title":"","content":"I'm agnostic. I've tried out lots of different religions and never found anything. But hey, maybe there's something to religion, and I have nothing but respect for those people who are able to have faith like that, as I just can't. Ultimately though, I generally don't bother with religion too much - I figure that I'll find out what's what after I'm dead, so why waste too much time worrying about it?<br /> <br /> That said, the religious/philosophical belief I've always liked the most was popular in Rome in the 2nd century or so, and tends to be referred to as &quot;Roman Universalism,&quot; and which led to the basic idea of Universalism.<br /> <br /> It basically posited that there was one deity out there, one supreme being, who was too big and too overwhelming for people to ever understand. But people would occasionally catch a glimpse of this being, though they would misinterpret it as something else. So some people might see the being's thumb and call it Thor. Or see it's left eye and call it Jupiter. Or see it's hair and call it Yahweh. Basically, every religion worships the same, vast, universal being, it's just that they call it different things and view it in different ways.<br /> <br /> So nobody's religion is 100% correct, but no religion is 100% wrong either. Every religion is both legitimate <i>and</i> wrong. This belief grew popular in Rome, as so many people were arriving there with their myriad deities and gods, and this was a useful philosophical idea to allow vastly different people with vastly different religious beliefs to coexist peacefully.<br /> <br /> So, even if I'm not religious and don't believe in the idea, I've always been a big fan of the concept at least.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T18:35:40-08:00","id":340317,"title":"","content":"I like a quote from the third book of the Mistborn series (which if you like fantasy and haven't read it yet, I highly recommend). A character spends most of the book trying to find a 'true' religion, but keeps dismissing them based on them not fitting observed evidence, or logical inconsistencies, or whatever. Later on, he realises that even if a religion is not empirically correct, that doesn't mean it doesn't have value, and that the <i>faith</i> it provides and inspires in its followers makes it worthy. As he put it &quot;They may not be true, but they contained truth&quot;.<br /> <br /> Of course, a lot of people do believe that their religion <i>is</i> empirically true. And hey, good for them, maybe they see something I don't. I don't claim to be all knowing, which means I might be wrong. But even if a given religion isn't empirically, factually correct, still has value.<br /> <br /> This is part of what bugs me about what I call Jerkoff Atheists. These are the specific atheists that don't just believe in no god/religion/whatever the hell atheists believe (it seems to vary on the individual), but they seem so offended at the idea of other people believing that they go out of their way to challenge their beliefs and try to enforce their views on them. What value does that have for society? What benefit is there? At least with evangelical Christians (or any evangelical individual, but Christians are an easy example) they're often doing it out of genuine concern for the persons soul or whatever. Not that it's not still incredibly annoying, but at least there's a shred of point. Jerkoff Atheists? Totally pointless to society at large, or the targeted individual.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T19:34:06-08:00","id":340366,"title":"","content":"I avoided this thread for a while as I feel one particular person long since on ignore was attempting to intimidate me from discussing such topics but well...I'm not going to be intimiated by someone, let alone someone on the internet.<br /> <br /> Atheist here. Parents had religious/semi religious upbringings. Those being my mother being what she referred to herself as a &quot;recovering Catholic.&quot; and in general spoke traumatized about it, describing it as an experience through the 40's-60's as &quot;They made you feel guilty about everything.&quot; While my father was raised Catholic in our Italian side of the family. He's never really been much of a religious person though I know he's had thoughts here and there. He saw a priest when my mother was dying,etc. Though for the most part that side of my family is pretty much just a case of &quot;We are Catholic when it's convenient.&quot; I only mention as part of explanation that I was not raised religious. My other decided when I was born not to baptize me as she felt it was wrong to baptize a child and that a person should get to make that choice when the are old enough to make that decision.<br /> <br /> Thankfully she was always supportive of me and never a bit of issue with me being atheist and we'd have discussions about religion from time to time. While of course later ideas through education,etc brought me to ideas about thinks like the social contract, humanist,etc atheist came quickly to me and it did not take me long to realize I was what is referred to in a lot of books as a &quot;strong atheist.&quot; Though these days what I mostly like to go on as is a humanist, secularist and anti-theist. Even living in the States I have to admit thanking that beyond an incident or two in the Marine Corps, as well as some rather hilarious moments involving a mega church in TN I've avoided the negative incidents involving atheism and theism clashing in America. There have been some touching moments in that though. A friend I made from Texas training asked me to come with him to Catholic services while we were in Infantry week at Pendleton. I still have a soft spot for Catholics in general despite despising the RCC as an entity. We went, it was fun and it made him feel better. Not long after the events of 911 I was going on a walk up a busy road I would take to go to work. Long walk,about three miles for the usual circuit to my job and I spot some teenagers carrying a pretty heavy American flag on a wooden pole. I walk beside em, ask the kid carrying it if he was getting tired and needed a spell. He thanked me and let me hold it for a while. After a long walk wrapping back around towards where i live they invited me to a local church in the area called Belview Baptist. It has a reputation among people in the city, a lot of teenagers sarcastically call it Six Flags Over Jesus. Ironically the future mother of my daughter's grandmother goes there. It wasn't for a service but a sort of game day thing there. I went a day later. People were nice but the way I tell people it's nice in a pod people sort of way.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is part of what bugs me about what I call Jerkoff Atheists. These are the specific atheists that don't just believe in no god/religion/whatever the hell atheists believe (it seems to vary on the individual), but they seem so offended at the idea of other people believing that they go out of their way to challenge their beliefs and try to enforce their views on them. What value does that have for society? What benefit is there? At least with evangelical Christians (or any evangelical individual, but Christians are an easy example) they're often doing it out of genuine concern for the persons soul or whatever. Not that it's not still incredibly annoying, but at least there's a shred of point. Jerkoff Atheists? Totally pointless to society at large, or the targeted individual. </div> </div> </div>In anything, public or otherwise that involves people interacting with other people and trying to persuade them of something. There are right and wrong ways to do it. Socially polite and socially impolite,etc. But the idea that all such interactions are both &quot;pointless to society at large...&quot; or cannot also involve a feeling of genuine concern(which you will note does not give any certainty that the person will handle the situation well, there are plenty of examples of poor, if genuine evangelizing). To your benefit, I'll assume you're NOT making a poorly made generalization and really only relegating that to the worst sort of behavior of as you put it, Jerkoff Atheists\u2122.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T19:58:55-08:00","id":340402,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=340366#post340366\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In anything, public or otherwise that involves people interacting with other people and trying to persuade them of something. There are right and wrong ways to do it. Socially polite and socially impolite,etc. But the idea that all such interactions are both &quot;pointless to society at large...&quot; or cannot also involve a feeling of genuine concern(which you will note does not give any certainty that the person will handle the situation well, there are plenty of examples of poor, if genuine evangelizing). To your benefit, I'll assume you're NOT making a poorly made generalization and really only relegating that to the worst sort of behavior of as you put it, Jerkoff Atheists\u2122.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, yes, that is what I was doing, specifically talking about the really loud, obnoxious and annoying kind. I'm sure most atheists, even strong atheists, are like most people: pretty cool if you treat them like human beings. I was specifically talking about the minority who specifically go after people with religious beliefs and harangue them for no reason other than because they're (the haranguer) dicks.<br /> <br /> Sorry if that wasn't clear.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T20:01:31-08:00","id":340404,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=340402#post340402\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ah, yes, that is what I was doing, specifically talking about the really loud, obnoxious and annoying kind. I'm sure most atheists, even strong atheists, are like most people: pretty cool if you treat them like human beings. I was specifically talking about the minority who specifically go after people with religious beliefs and harangue them for no reason other than because they're (the haranguer) dicks.<br /> <br /> Sorry if that wasn't clear.</div> </div> </div> </div>I thought you were, the tm was a wink towards that heh. I would consider myself lucky that in addition to avoiding nasty religious run ins, I've never had the displeasure of running into, net or otherwise, the proverbial angry atheist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-14T20:24:25-08:00","id":340442,"title":"","content":"Only real experience I've had with &quot;pushy&quot; religious people was one guy who belonged to a particularly evangelical church in my city and worked with the Red Frog Crew (for those that don't know, they're a group of [mostly] Christians who you can ask to show up to events who provide free water and red lolly frogs to people, the idea being to act as positive role models by not drinking, as well as general support and education. Vast majority are incredibly cool people, and they never push the religious angle) at a party my college threw. Got to talking to him, and he convinced me to come to one of his churches services. I was very clear that I was not a Christian, and much though I respect the faith, I do not share it, but was willing to come along as an educational experience. Also because he wouldn't leave me alone about it; I have my suspicions about why, but they're not relevant here.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I went along, and, well, it was an experience. It was one of those 'musical' churches, the ones that try to stimulate a sense of... well, religious ecstasy is too strong a term, but I can't think of a better one. All the way through the initial conversation, to the trip there, even his behaviour while there, it was clear he expected me to fall over and convert on the spot, which of course I did not. Even on the trip back (I didn't have my license at the time, so he drove me), he was still trying, even though I pointed out that even if I did agree with his perspective, faith isn't something you can easily choose or turn on or off.<br /> <br /> On the scale of &quot;pushy religious people&quot; he's pretty mild, I know. But I've known a lot of Jerkoff Atheists<sup>TM</sup>, so naturally I find they annoy me more, simply due to me being exposed to them more. In comparison, most religious people I've known have been very cool people, so naturally the distribution of cool to jerk is kind of skewed towards that end of the spectrum. But I've known some cool atheists as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-12-23T07:07:06-08:00","id":348253,"title":"","content":"My experience is opposite since there is no shortage of pushy religious people here and many Atheists are closeted. If they came out of closet then Catholic population would be reduced from 90 % to its real value of 30 - 40 %.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T10:20:28-08:00","id":409081,"title":"","content":"Hello, I know it's not a place for debate but I would want the opinions of religious people about it :<br /> <br /> I just don't understand why it's an issue in this world, I mean where the whole concept come from except some books and traditions ? would we even had the concept of religion without it ? I mean i wasn't raised religiously (without being raised as an atheist, religion was just not a thing in my education and the best description of my parents about it would be &quot;apatheists&quot;, if I was baptized roman catholic because it's what everyone do where I live ) and I would never think about things like religion and god(s) by my own, so it don't seems really natural to me (I know that many religious people thinks being religious is natural but from my experience, it's not the case)<br /> <br /> I describe myself as atheist because I think that agnosticism is too much...passive : i don't consider &quot;believe in god&quot; and &quot;don't believe in god&quot; as two equals possibilities since for me, it's to those who affirms something to prove it more than those who don't believe in this thing; and I don't think It's really possible to be apatheist in a so religious world.<br /> <br /> I have nothing about religious people but I don't understand them : how it's possible to believe so strongly in something without proofs.<br /> And how you know it's the right religion ? I mean..well when someone ask about it generaly he is answered by an citation of the Bible that said &quot;Jesus is the true path&quot; or something like that but without offense, it's a bit senseless for someone who don't already believe in the Bible and Jesus and you're far the only ones to think like that : muslims said Islam is the true religion because it's written in the Quran, Jews thinks they are the chosen people because its written in the Tora, ect...why i would believe you rather than them ? Why i would believe them rather than you ?<br /> Some others speak about religious experience but it exist in all religions and many good people die without this kind of experience and says &quot;if someone is a good-will man, he will find God&quot; is quite an unprobable and easy answer. (I doubt that NEVER a good-will man is dead before find God, by principle we can assume anyone can die at every time; and it's too easy to affirm that those who fails to see the Truth were not honest enough)<br /> <br /> Some others said that the Truth must be not so much evident otherwise there is not free-will and the good choice must be more difficult to take than the bad ones but there is a difference between that and makes it completly unknowledgeable and so random (if you can't know what is the good choice, it's not really free-will and more of a gage)<br /> <br /> And the most weird thing is that what is your religion is in 99% of the cases just a demographic thing (see statistic : there is religious map of the world) : most people believe in what they have been raised and in most places in the world, your religion is not even a choice.<br /> And have you already seen someone's who wasn't raised with any idea of God becomes religious ? All converts I know had deep roted idea of the Christian God and were almost christians in denial.<br /> <br /> <br /> I don't grasp how a &quot;true&quot; unbeliever can convert and why.<br /> <br /> And if a religion is true does it means 99% of those who believe in this Religion goes in the Heavens because they have been raised in the good religion even if they wouldn't even considerate the &quot;good religion&quot; if they have been raised in an another religion ?<br /> And 99% of those who believed in a wrong religion goes to hell because they have been raised in a wrong one even if they weren't less goods or open-minded than those who have been raised in the good religion ?<br /> <br /> I don't think I'm closed-minded : I have reconsiderate my position several times, try to understand believers and all that; I don't think I'm smarter than most people so I'm a bit puzzled about the fact of being in a extrem minority in a world where almost everyone don't thinks at all like me but it's still the same problem.<br /> <br /> I hope I was not offensing; if you have an opinion about it, it would be great.<br /> <br /> Thanks. (Sorry for the bad english, it's not my native language)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/343-suleri-drals","name":"Suleri Drals","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar343_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"244"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T11:08:50-08:00","id":409133,"title":"","content":"Why have this thread? <br /> <br /> I am more than willing to talk about what I believe in if someone asks me, but not here. Not on the boards. We all have our various beliefs and are not always going to agree even if we have the same beliefs Too many times it comes down to bashing someone else's beliefs. <br /> <br /> This is the kind of subject that has started wars and mass exterminations of non believers in what you believe. Sadly my fellow followers of the same belief have done that in the past. <br /> <br /> We just need to treat each other with respect. Help each other. Do no harm to each other. In the end that is what all beliefs say in the end.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4949-nene-richards","name":"Nene Richards","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4949_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T11:12:34-08:00","id":409137,"title":"","content":"I was raised into Orthodox Christianity, but lost my faith in that particular flavour of Christianity due to its narrow criteria of who makes it into heaven. After that, I figured I would explore more spiritual creeds and started reading up on Sufi Islam but by that time I witnessed enough evil in the world to effectively run out of spiritual fuel. Now I'm 25, facing a premature mid-life crisis, where every and all parts of my identity are constantly under fire by my cynical subconscious. Feels near-impossible to find a way out and not just on religious or spiritual terms.<br /> <br /> Feels like for as long as this crisis lasts, I am stuck in a slump. Brain always feels like it runs on energy saving mode, and every day seems like I have less and less energy for stuff. Recently I discovered Hermeticism and the Qabalah, which feel like the building blocks for the kind of person I will emerge out of this all. We'll see... Right now my gods are caffeine and nicotine, for what it's worth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T11:42:14-08:00","id":409183,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nene Richards</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=409133#post409133\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why have this thread?</div> </div> </div> </div>Look further back in the history of this topic on this forum and you will see lots of answers to this question.<br /> <br /> Most of those answers come in two varieties. One, those wishing to discuss their beliefs and defend them to the death. Two, those who question the necessity and validity of the topic in the first place.<br /> <br /> So, why do we have this thread? Meh, fuck if I know.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-17T23:16:16-08:00","id":409736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Suleri Drals</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=409081#post409081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello, I know it's not a place for debate but I would want the opinions of religious people about it :<br /> <br /> I just don't understand why it's an issue in this world, I mean where the whole concept come from except some books and traditions ?</div> </div> </div> </div>I do not consider myself to be a religious person, as I have been raised in a culture in which that category, by its definition, does not really apply. I also would not easily subscribe to being atheist or agnostic for the same reason. My opinion is that it is because of these categories and the quest to find similarities and differences between the many belief systems of humankind, that causes the issues, as you say, in the world. For example, the hunt for more and more ways to differentiate your own religion from every other belief system in order to convince other's that your belief's are correct. Another example would be to be inclusive of all religious belief and address all the similarities inherent in the faiths to prove that your belief's are correct. Being right and correct about one's faith is an important factor because it would be bad to be wrong. I don't understand myself why being correct is good and being incorrect is wrong. Some people have told me it is human nature, but I don't know what that is. No one has been able to tell me what human nature is because they don't know either. <br /> <br /> It is because of all the assumptions, such as my example above, that there are issues in the world. Certainly, what you say is true. Religions have books and tradition, but they also have people, and everyone will probably answer your question differently.<br /> <br /> Because everyone else has revealed what they believe in, I will reveal mine. I believe in the self (which includes both myself and yourself). Of course, I am also a part of Confucian, Buddhist, and Daoist tradition (with some Shinto and Hindu elements now that I have come to know them better). But those are Dharmas, Shukyos, and Zhuxues, none of which contextually translates to the word religion very well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>How can I know if what I claim I know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I might not know? How can I know if what I claim I don't know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I do know?<br /> -Zhuangzi","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1715-ostarion","name":"Ostarion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1715_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"305"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-21T16:35:26-08:00","id":414600,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nene Richards</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=409133#post409133\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why have this thread?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's difficult to ignore something that seems to deal with over 90% of the world population.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Suleri Drals</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=409081#post409081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't grasp how a &quot;true&quot; unbeliever can convert and why.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you want an example: <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antony_Flew\" target=\"_blank\">Antony Flew</a><br /> <i><br /> For much of his career Flew was known as a strong advocate of atheism, arguing that one should presuppose atheism until empirical evidence of a God surfaces. He also criticized the idea of life after death, the free will defense to the problem of evil, and the meaningfulness of the concept of God. In 2003 he was one of the signers of the Humanist Manifesto. However, in 2004 he stated an allegiance to deism, more specifically a belief in the Aristotelian God. He stated that in keeping his lifelong commitment to go where the evidence leads, he now believed in the existence of a god.</i><br /> -----------------<br /> <br /> That's not a provocation for debate. Just putting it out there. If you decide to read the full article you may &quot;grasp&quot; how it happens, at least to one individual.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-21T20:15:54-08:00","id":414797,"title":"","content":"Lapsed Lutheran, if such nomenclature allows. I've been described as an Agnostic Theist. That is, I believe in a higher power, generally benevolent if a little eccentric, but I also believe in concepts like evolution, science, physics, all that jazz.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-21T20:57:07-08:00","id":414814,"title":"","content":"Religion has been a mess in my life so I have ignored it and focused mainly on spirituality. I was catholic when I was young until I decided to go on a spiritual journey and ended up becoming a pagan. Truth is I had complications with the Catholic Church and there structural beliefs. I simply found them ridiculous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2360-cecoyotl","name":"Cecoyotl","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2360_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-22T13:57:54-08:00","id":415627,"title":"","content":"Gnostic Atheist - I firmly believe that there is nothing to believe in.<br /> <br /> I was raised Orthodox Christian, but at around 7 it became absolutely, undeniably clear to me that when I die, there will be nothing left of me - no empty void, no recognition of there being nothing, I'll just completely cease to exist. And I'm fine with that.<br /> <br /> Since then I've had a few other realizations about patterns and the nature of the universe, and it all reinforces my one core belief: we are the universe trying to figure itself out. We don't just live in it, we are it. Not in a &quot;spiritual new-age The Secret the-universe-listens-to-you&quot; kind of way, just in a matter-of-fact &quot;our atoms are the universe's atoms and there is no real distinction&quot; sort of way. That we think, that we move, that we recognize and produce patterns, that we build and transport resources from point A to point B as firing neurons or vascular systems do, like some sort of ... God-Machine (pun very much intended). All those things <i>are</i> the cosmos, as well as being galaxies, black holes and supernovae. That we walk around thinking we <i>aren't</i> part of a greater system is akin to skin-cells or brain-cells not understanding that they live in a body, and that they <i>are</i> that body despite their own perceived individuality.<br /> <br /> People usually try to argue with &quot;But we're more than the sum of our parts!&quot; to which I can't help but answer &quot;then you don't understand how they fit together&quot;. The Cosmos is more interesting, complicated and fascinating than any magic we try to layer over it.<br /> <br /> Despite the humor, that actually is my current working theory.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:lucida sans unicode\">Bearer of the legacy of Trauma Bear<br /> Need a dice-roller? Check out <a href=\"http://bit.ly/dicemat\" target=\"_blank\">Dicemat</a>.</span>","edited":{"userid":43,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/43-fata-ku","user":"Fata-Ku","date":". 02-22-2015, 05:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/43-fata-ku","name":"Fata-Ku","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar43_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2240"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T17:39:46-08:00","id":423623,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=306953#post306953\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Christianity supports the idea of redemption and being kind to one another, Buddhism is about letting go of attachments, ...</div> </div> </div> </div>Buddhism is also about redemption and being kind to one another. (And to be fair, reading Paul and other very early Christian writers gives one the impression that they were largely about letting go of attachments, but that got downplayed over time.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=310993#post310993\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I remember the first time I read Tao Te Ching. It was around the same time I was reading Buddhism, and I remember thinking it's a mix of Terry Pratchett, 1984, and Buddhism.<br /> <br /> (Yes, I know the influence was most likely the other way around with Buddhism.)</div> </div> </div> </div>When it comes to Chinese Buddhism and Daoism, the influence runs both ways across a number of centuries. For one thing, Daoism borrowed a lot of the structure of Buddhism, since Buddhism was the more organized religion to begin with, while Chinese Buddhists borrowed a lot of Daoist terminology in order to discuss concepts that Daoists had already developed in the Chinese language. Aside from that, I'm pretty sure both traditions came to a lot of the same conclusions independently because, well, they're true. The problems of dualistic thinking, for example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-28T21:03:56-08:00","id":423800,"title":"","content":"Christian atheist and mystic here.ho<br /> <br /> It's a tough thing to explain in a brief paragraph or two, but the basic idea is that you really can't identify what God is, but rather what God isn't. I'm probably most Blakean in my approach - I identify most traditional concepts of God as being identical to Satan(&quot;though you are worshipped by the names of Jesus and Jehovah, you are the son of morning&quot;) and I think one has to go through the experience of the death of one's satanic &quot;Selfhood&quot; before one can really know Christ. I'm also really attracted to more academic approaches like death of God theology, transtheism, weak theology, and Christian existentialism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problems of dualistic thinking, for example. </div> </div> </div>Thomas Altizer would say that Eastern religion, though it's dialectical, is really a step backwards into eternity and a primordial totality, and because of that it's is really irrelevant towards contemporary man. Thoughts on that?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thinking as I do that the Creator of this World is a very Cruel Being &amp; being a Worshipper of Christ, I cannot help saying: &quot;the Son, O how unlike the Father!&quot; First God Almighty comes with a Thump on the Head. Then Jesus Christ comes with a balm to heal it. - William Blake, Christian mystic and visionary","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/774-pilgrim","name":"Pilgrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar774_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T05:06:42-08:00","id":423998,"title":"","content":"I'm reminded of the Cathars that viewed the material world as having been created by evil, and that having a child in it was giving the devil another hostage...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T08:02:50-08:00","id":424167,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=423998#post423998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm reminded of the Cathars that viewed the material world as having been created by evil, and that having a child in it was giving the devil another hostage...</div> </div> </div> </div>I view the world as absolutely evil and the fall as being creation itself. I have an incredibly negative and depressing worldview","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thinking as I do that the Creator of this World is a very Cruel Being &amp; being a Worshipper of Christ, I cannot help saying: &quot;the Son, O how unlike the Father!&quot; First God Almighty comes with a Thump on the Head. Then Jesus Christ comes with a balm to heal it. - William Blake, Christian mystic and visionary","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/774-pilgrim","name":"Pilgrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar774_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"52"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-01T20:18:30-08:00","id":424804,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pilgrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=423800#post423800\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thomas Altizer would say that Eastern religion, though it's dialectical, is really a step backwards into eternity and a primordial totality, and because of that it's is really irrelevant towards contemporary man. Thoughts on that?</div> </div> </div> </div>My initial thought is that &quot;Eastern religion&quot; is an incoherent phrase. Buddhism and Daoism might have a couple of key points of contact, but they're not ultimately the same thing, and they certainly don't represent some general trend that covers all of Asian religious practice. If anything, they're pretty odd.<br /> <br /> Now, philosophical Daoism can be said to deal with something that might be called a primordial totality, in the sense that the Dao is regarded as what there really <i>is</i>. It's the true nature of the self, the phenomenal world, etc. Not sure how relevant any of that is for actual Daoist religious practice, though. It's probably different for each individual sect. And more importantly, not sure how it's a step backwards. Backwards from <i>what</i>? If the idea is that Daoist thought devalues actual human experience in favor of vague abstractions, then I don't think that's a fair reading--like analogous developments in Greek philosophy, their theories about the true nature of the cosmos are really only relevant insofar as they have ethical implications. But if it were true, I would indeed call that counterproductive.<br /> <br /> Buddhist thought lacks even those grounds for selfhood: there's no sense in which a totality is any more legitimately &quot;real&quot; than an individual entity, and the focus of the actual religion is very much on the experiences of actual people rather than abstractions. Nor is there anything that could be called &quot;primordial&quot; or &quot;eternal,&quot; since reality is regarded as being perpetually constructed in the present. And the whole tradition is about as humanistic as they come, being constructed from top to bottom with no purpose other than to help people to improve themselves, fulfill their potential, and be happy. As such, I don't see how Altizer's statement would apply.<br /> <br /> Of course, it's worth pointing out that monism is not the sole alternative to dualism, and neither of the above traditions is monistic. Laozi seems to reify the Dao, but it's not really a thing so much as a process, and seemingly opposite characteristics are ultimately unreal in that they're byproducts of it and of biases in human cognition. Buddhist thought is more circumspect when it comes to reification but is otherwise fairly similar in terms of practical application: things like good and bad, high and low, hot and cold are conceptual categories that aren't ultimately real, but it doesn't follow that there's a single thing that <i>is </i>ultimately real, including the totality of all of the above. Perhaps that's where Altizer got confused. But really, he was writing at a time when knowledge of Buddhism was pretty scarce in the West, and what information people did have was often wildly inaccurate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T02:01:30-08:00","id":424972,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pilgrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=423800#post423800\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thomas Altizer would say that Eastern religion, though it's dialectical, is really a step backwards into eternity and a primordial totality, and because of that it's is really irrelevant towards contemporary man. Thoughts on that?</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say that there is nothing wrong with that opinion and there is nothing right about it either. He is right in only how it applies to himself and those who believe him and he is wrong when applied to anyone else that does not know or , perhaps, does not consider themselves to be contemporary. In the end, there is no such thing as eastern religion except in western discourse and academia, so he isn't really talking about anything but himself anyway.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>How can I know if what I claim I know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I might not know? How can I know if what I claim I don't know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I do know?<br /> -Zhuangzi","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1715-ostarion","name":"Ostarion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1715_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"305"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T03:22:37-08:00","id":424988,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Flag</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=423623#post423623\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Buddhism is also about redemption and being kind to one another. (And to be fair, reading Paul and other very early Christian writers gives one the impression that they were largely about letting go of attachments, but that got downplayed over time.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I was simplifying for the sake of clear communication and illustration. Obviously with any ideas as complex as most religions you can't simply reduce them down to one idea or value without horrendously oversimplifying them to the point of absurdity. Hell, the idea that you can draw the same lesson from multiple sources makes my argument even stronger, in my view.<br /> <br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pilgrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=424167#post424167\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I view the world as absolutely evil and the fall as being creation itself. I have an incredibly negative and depressing worldview</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see that view, but even when I consider it, I keep coming back to &quot;if that's the case, then what?&quot; The most obvious, if far from the only, logical progression is that doing anything is pointless, or even potentially 'evil' itself, and that it's impossible to change it. Which very well may be the case, but I cannot believe that the immense complexity of existence, let alone life, is without some purpose, even if that purpose is created ourselves.<br /> <br /> If nothing else, I find it difficult enough to get out of bed in the morning as it is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T10:06:08-08:00","id":425371,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=424988#post424988\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can see that view, but even when I consider it, I keep coming back to &quot;if that's the case, then what?&quot; The most obvious, if far from the only, logical progression is that doing anything is pointless, or even potentially 'evil' itself, and that it's impossible to change it. Which very well may be the case, but I cannot believe that the immense complexity of existence, let alone life, is without some purpose, even if that purpose is created ourselves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, if my life is purposeless, does it not behoove me to give it purpose? And the world is only as evil as we allow it to be. Frequently painful, yes, but not inherently morally wicked. Making ourselves and the world better than they are might be difficult--it might even seem impossible--but it's still a worthy endeavor. And if we don't try, then we know for certain that nothing will ever improve. So if it's a choice between wallowing in depravity and despair, or at least <i>trying </i>for something better, then the choice would seem to be obvious.<br /> <br /> I would have more respect for Pascal's wager if it had been couched in those terms. Belief or nonbelief in God isn't really the point. The point is that between <i>certain </i>failure and <i>possible </i>success, taking a chance on the latter is always the smart bet, since losing doesn't leave you any worse off than if you'd picked the other option. There is a sense in which hope has no downside.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-02T12:23:39-08:00","id":425538,"title":"","content":"Except then it wouldn't be Pascal's Wager, it would be the Anti-Bernoulli's Wager.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-03T08:13:24-08:00","id":426582,"title":"","content":"*shrugs* A belief that someone upstairs may be watching you 24/7 isn't always a bad thing. (except when I use the toilet.) Keeps my conscience in check.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-03T08:31:27-08:00","id":426594,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=426582#post426582\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*shrugs* A belief that someone upstairs may be watching you 24/7 isn't always a bad thing. (except when I use the toilet.) Keeps my conscience in check.</div> </div> </div> </div>*The Eye grins*","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-04T02:42:26-08:00","id":427551,"title":"","content":"Course, the problem with Pascals Wager, or equivalent arguments, is that it ignores alternate positions. Classically, that there are other traditions, so it's not a binary yes-no.<br /> <br /> Somewhat more on-point, however, is the idea of how 'evil' is defined. Can something be inherently evil? Inherent implies, at least, that choice is not a thing, and without choice, can something have the moral dimension implied by the term? If we assume that reality itself is not sentient, then that implies it cannot make choice, which would imply that it therefore cannot be moral or immoral. Really, the best you can argue is that it's antithetical to human life or perspective, a la Lovecraft.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, that argument has like a dozen assumptions built into it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-04T06:30:00-08:00","id":427662,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=426582#post426582\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*shrugs* A belief that someone upstairs may be watching you 24/7 isn't always a bad thing. (except when I use the toilet.) Keeps my conscience in check.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would much prefer people strive to be good even when no one's watching.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-04T08:05:19-08:00","id":427733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>etherial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=427662#post427662\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would much prefer people strive to be good even when no one's watching.</div> </div> </div> </div>Character is who you are when no one else is watching. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-04T12:55:15-08:00","id":428015,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=427551#post427551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Course, the problem with Pascals Wager, or equivalent arguments, is that it ignores alternate positions. Classically, that there are other traditions, so it's not a binary yes-no.<br /> <br /> Somewhat more on-point, however, is the idea of how 'evil' is defined. Can something be inherently evil? Inherent implies, at least, that choice is not a thing, and without choice, can something have the moral dimension implied by the term? If we assume that reality itself is not sentient, then that implies it cannot make choice, which would imply that it therefore cannot be moral or immoral. Really, the best you can argue is that it's antithetical to human life or perspective, a la Lovecraft.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, that argument has like a dozen assumptions built into it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was thinking on this some months back. My thoughts can be summarized something like this...<br /> <br /> Broadly the definitions of good/evil that we seem to keep coming back to is the model of selflessness or selfishness. The dedication of resources to the advantage of others is seen as doing good, particularly if those people are lacking in those resources. Evil is the collecting of resources to the self, particularly if those resources are gathered from those that would then be suffering from the lack of that resource.<br /> <br /> The expression of pure evil would then be the destruction/elimination of resources for no gain at all. And it's important to note that fame, including notoriety or outright hatred, is a thing that can be gained... so as an example, the asshole rich guy that burns a pile of cash in front of the starving poor of Oakland just because he can isn't doing a pure evil act, because he's gaining what he wants from it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T00:02:42-08:00","id":428760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=428015#post428015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I was thinking on this some months back. My thoughts can be summarized something like this...<br /> <br /> Broadly the definitions of good/evil that we seem to keep coming back to is the model of selflessness or selfishness. The dedication of resources to the advantage of others is seen as doing good, particularly if those people are lacking in those resources. Evil is the collecting of resources to the self, particularly if those resources are gathered from those that would then be suffering from the lack of that resource.<br /> <br /> The expression of pure evil would then be the destruction/elimination of resources for no gain at all. And it's important to note that fame, including notoriety or outright hatred, is a thing that can be gained... so as an example, the asshole rich guy that burns a pile of cash in front of the starving poor of Oakland just because he can isn't doing a pure evil act, because he's gaining what he wants from it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Given the fact that everything people do is driven by motive, and thus everything we do should, at least in theory, result in us getting something out of it, doesn't that then imply that humans are incapable of pure evil, according to that formulation?<br /> <br /> And, if we invert it, and say that pure good according to that viewpoint is doing something that benefits others with literally no benefit to the self, then the same reasoning implies that we are also incapable of pure good?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T02:01:02-08:00","id":428870,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that's the takeaway from that reasoning. Pure good is imposible as long as you derive satisfaction from doing good. Wich in my opinion is stupid and bullshit (ditto for &quot;pure evil&quot;).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T02:24:49-08:00","id":428886,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't say it's stupid. We may not be able to achieve <i>pure</i> good, but that doesn't mean that the good that we do achieve is less good. The people we help aren't any less helped because we take pleasure in doing so, any more than the people we hurt aren't any less hurt because we feel bad.<br /> <br /> Personally, I find formulations of &quot;pure&quot; good/evil to be an interesting philosophical exercise, but practically meaningless. It's our actions that matter and have a moral dimension, not our feelings or our thoughts. I can sit and think of the most horrific and twisted things in the universe, and while that might say something about my character, ultimately nobody has been helped (unless doing so helps me release feelings without acting on them, thus preventing me hurting someone in the future) or hurt (unless doing so pushes me towards indulging those fantasies) by those thoughts. And even if those conditions are met, it's the actions that follow that are relevant to the equation.<br /> <br /> It's like the old hat about two people giving money to a charity. One is a millionaire, and gives $10,000, the other is poor, and gives $10. Often, people will say that the poor person is more moral, because the amount represented a larger sacrifice. Personally, I think that's idiotic. The poor person might be more <i>admirable</i>, and we might be able to infer something about their character, but the millionaire has done more good, all else being equal. So, in a consequentialist perspective, the millionaire did the more moral thing. His millions in no way lessen the good his donation can now do. This is especially true once you factor in that these acts are entirely voluntary: the millionaire has no obligation whatsoever to give anything, so he made the choice. Maybe it's a choice the poor person couldn't have made, but the choice was still made, and more good was still done as a result.<br /> <br /> Course, the reverse is also true. If the millionaire used his $10,000 to cause some harm, like funding organised crime or something, and the poor person did the same action with $10, then odds-on the millionaire caused more harm, and as such did a more immoral act. A millionaire who doesn't give anything to anyone, for good or ill, and a poor person who does the same, are exactly the same, morally speaking, all else being equal, since their actions have had exactly the same impact: zero. Again, we can infer different things about their characters, because those assessments have to exist in context, but morally? It's the outcome that's important, not the motivation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T02:45:29-08:00","id":428894,"title":"","content":"Thing is, when you set parameters for &quot;purity&quot; of any kind, you automatically lessen anything on that scale below those parameters. Saying that you cannot perform a pure good act as long as you derive any kind of satisfaction or utility from it seems unecesary restrictive. Does that definition have any actual utility besides deriding literally any act of good any person can or has ever performed?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T02:56:10-08:00","id":428898,"title":"","content":"I think we're arguing the same point from different perspectives","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T03:03:10-08:00","id":428900,"title":"","content":"We very well might be. I still can't shake my initial reaction of considering the 10$ donative by the poor person inherently better despite the reduced utility derived by society from it if compared to the rich man's 10.000$. That's what you get from a catholic upbringing I guess.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T03:26:46-08:00","id":428931,"title":"","content":"Well, one can make an argument that since it requires greater sacrifice, then it is an inherently more noble act. Personally, I think that viewpoint is flawed to the point of rendering it unworkable in a practical sense, and potentially even actively harmful in some particular contexts.<br /> <br /> Catholic (actually, Christian in general) theology tends to support it, I believe (?), with the story of the talens and suchforth, and the argument is not without merit. If one accepts that since we are, uniquely among known life-forms, capable of imagining a world different than it is, then we have a responsibility to try to realise this vision to the best of our ability, then logically to whom much is given much is expected. Course, this does tend to easily lend itself to living in bad faith, morally at least. It's like my country, which is currently debating the extent and type of action we should take to reduce carbon emissions. Leaving aside that debate for a moment, a lot of people, not without justification, point out that given the comparative emissions of places like China and the US, anything we do is insignificant, so the idea that we should bend over backwards to affect nothing is ludicrous. Now, in theory, assuming we accept the existence of man-fuelled climate change, and also assume that we can affect the unfolding of it through the controlling of carbon emissions, than anything we can do is good, regardless of others actions. Course, when such actions may result in things like damage of the economy, loss of jobs, increased cost of living and other such things, then the equation becomes more complex.<br /> <br /> (Not saying any of those assumptions or projected conclusions are accurate or inaccurate. Just pointing out the mental processes, and some of the complexities of the decision-making process.)<br /> <br /> Like I said earlier, whenever people start talking about whether something is right or wrong, or social ills, or anything like that, I always bring it back to &quot;what can I, personally, do?&quot; Can I control what other people do with their money? No, and I do not believe that I should, valuing free will as I do. But I can control my own actions and decisions. Can I afford to give some money to charity, when the share market is nice to me? Sometimes, and I do. Does it have an impact? No idea, probably not. Does it harm? No idea. Ultimately we have to work from imperfect information and best-guess. So, to bring it back to my original point, saying the millionaire's actions are less moral just makes it less likely that they will choose to engage in such actions. I'm not saying a rich person should have a parade thrown for them, but devaluing their socially-positive actions isn't constructive either. Plus, it means &quot;why should I give money to the Red Cross, when those Evil Corporations are doing so much harm in comparison?&quot;, which means many people won't give to the Red Cross, which would seriously hamper their ability to do good, which would mean people who need help wouldn't get it, which would be bad.<br /> <br /> Actions, consequences, in a context of imperfect information and limited control. All we can control is our own actions, and ultimately, that's the only choice that matters, and where morality values have to be based.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T03:39:59-08:00","id":428933,"title":"","content":"Note that said millionare may derive other kind of utility form said acts, so considering them more or less moral may not have such a direct impact on the choice to perform them or not. It may be seen as good publicity, or may benefit from some tax reductions, etc, etc. I mean, morality may not even take a part on the choice, it may very well be a completely calculated and logical choice to do so, cost-benefit wise.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T11:48:10-08:00","id":429080,"title":"","content":"In the Buddhist view, there is ultimately no giver or receiver. What matters is whether the act was carried out with benevolent intentions and is likely to result in favorable consequences, or whether it reinforces negative habits and is likely to result in unfavorable consequences. The idea that an act could not be good because it somehow benefitted the doer would just be seen as bizarre. Good brings benefits, or else it would not be good.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, there is ultimately no evil, just people acting under a variety of delusions, thinking that their actions will bring them happiness but being sorely mistaken.<br /> <br /> The trick is that things that appear to benefit you at the expense of others are not good things, since they reinforce the delusion that you are ultimately separate from other people, and that happiness is a zero-sum game that can be won.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-05T21:24:55-08:00","id":429803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thorbes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=428933#post428933\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Note that said millionare may derive other kind of utility form said acts, so considering them more or less moral may not have such a direct impact on the choice to perform them or not. It may be seen as good publicity, or may benefit from some tax reductions, etc, etc. I mean, morality may not even take a part on the choice, it may very well be a completely calculated and logical choice to do so, cost-benefit wise.</div> </div> </div> </div>None of which means a damn thing to the kid who gets clothes that fit and a few hot meals because of it.<br /> <br /> Whether the giver intended to do good or not, good was done.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-06T04:34:12-08:00","id":430017,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that was my point, beliebe it or not. That was a common argument to devaluete the donatives of rich men that I'm quite tired of hearing: &quot;Of course he gave x-thousands$! That means nothing to him and he gets Tax reductions!.<br /> <br /> Ok, who the hell cares why he did as long as he did it? That money is helping people anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-06T13:13:28-08:00","id":430530,"title":"","content":"I've seen a little too much not to believe in the duality of good and evil. The epitome of evil, of which I believe is insanity, because there is a point which is incomprehensible to me. Not that I care to make much of it comprehensible to begin with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-09T21:55:08-07:00","id":435263,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=430530#post430530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've seen a little too much not to believe in the duality of good and evil. The epitome of evil, of which I believe is insanity, because there is a point which is incomprehensible to me. Not that I care to make much of it comprehensible to begin with.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I do think it's apt to describe evil as a sickness, I would argue that it's very important for us to understand why people do the things they do. We like to think we're not capable of doing stuff like that, but people are capable of all kinds of things under the right circumstances. And while we may not commit atrocities, we are very likely to be callous towards others and commit little, banal evils in the course of our daily lives, without ever thinking about it. And the difference is really just one of scale. If bad habits are cultivated, then all it takes is a moment of lowered inhibitions to do something terrible. Or a situation in which an entire social group is advocating it, making it seem licit. It happens to normal people every day.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/352-black-flag","name":"Black Flag","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar352_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T08:32:53-07:00","id":435752,"title":"","content":"When some people enjoy inflicting pain on others;<br /> When genocide is committed;<br /> When people feed off the misery of others;<br /> I find myself unwilling to understand it. It's a cancer that simply needs to be removed and buried.<br /> <br /> When they were cutting the right-hands off of young men/boys in certain parts of the African continent, just because I understand that they wanted to reduce the likelihood of them being a threat later in life, while still being productive in servitude, does not in any way make that less evil.<br /> <br /> Do I need to understand the likes of Jeffrey Dahmer?<br /> No.<br /> His fate however? Bitter irony? Poetic justice? Or in the context of the thread, perhaps even divine?<br /> *shrugs*","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T08:41:37-07:00","id":435756,"title":"","content":"Maybe it's the Taoist perspective, maybe the Absurdist perspective, but I can't get behind the concept of something being totally good or totally evil. There's too many ways to put positive spins on awful acts and too many ways to attribute selfish motivations to altruistic actions. No one can really know why someone chooses to do something unless you are in their heads.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T11:06:26-07:00","id":435974,"title":"","content":"It has occurred to me that all of us (theists or not) share a common problem. It seems like humans largely think of good and evil in objective, real term, at least at first brush or first thought. The problem then is that while humanity hold good and evil to be objectively real, its true content or expression elude agreement. While I will certainly grant that there is a lot of agreement about what is good and evil, it seems impossible to deny that their strong differences of opinion. I think it is clear that any system - like a religion - that defines what is good and evil lack external validity at least operationally to those that do not ascribe to it. As a Christian, this point has been made clear to me by many assorted atheist friends. However, what I do not think most of these friends realize is that this is true too for non-religious systems that use non-religious idea like reciprocity. We are all in the same boat, so to speak.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":790,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/790-cire","user":"Cire","date":".  typos 03-10-2015, 02:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/790-cire","name":"Cire","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"549"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T16:24:30-07:00","id":436396,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Papa Bear</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=435756#post435756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe it's the Taoist perspective, maybe the Absurdist perspective, but I can't get behind the concept of something being totally good or totally evil. There's too many ways to put positive spins on awful acts and too many ways to attribute selfish motivations to altruistic actions. No one can really know why someone chooses to do something unless you are in their heads.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed. &quot;For all have sinned and fallen short of the glory of God&quot;<br /> <br /> The line between good and evil runs through the heart of all people. All are tempted, and a person only becomes irredeemable when they choose not to atone for their transgressions.<br /> <br /> But there is evil; we know this. And so evil must be fought and punished, lest it run free and turn the earth into hell. But we must be willing to forgive, to give a fair chance for redemption.<br /> <br /> Nobody's perfect.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm feeling bluuuueeeee~","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/175-prometheus878","name":"Prometheus878","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar175_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1477"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T17:18:09-07:00","id":436441,"title":"","content":"I've difficulty believing in an ultimate evil/good ideal. For myself, I don't need to get into anyone's head to see what his/her actions are.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-10T19:10:51-07:00","id":436543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheus878</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=436396#post436396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Indeed. &quot;For all have sinned and fallen short of the glory of God&quot;<br /> <br /> The line between good and evil runs through the heart of all people. All are tempted, and a person only becomes irredeemable when they choose not to atone for their transgressions.<br /> <br /> But there is evil; we know this. And so evil must be fought and punished, lest it run free and turn the earth into hell. But we must be willing to forgive, to give a fair chance for redemption.<br /> <br /> Nobody's perfect.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Still voting Prometheus878 for president, fall 2016. I want a commander-in-chief that isn't afraid to fight evil using an army of giant robots.<br /> <br /> Go Promie, Go! <br /> (We'll just get you some fake documentation. My cousin Miguel wasn't born in East L.A., but his birth certificate says so. You know what I'm saying?)<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><span style=\"color:#0000CD\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>\u201cNobody is purely good or purely evil. Mo</b></span><b>st of us are in-between. There are moths that explore the day and butterflies that play at night.\u201d </b><br /> - Suzy Kassem, Rise Up and Salute The Sun<br /> <b>(She/Her)</b></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/380-azraelfirestorm","name":"AzraelFirestorm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar380_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-11T01:26:10-07:00","id":436822,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=430530#post430530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've seen a little too much not to believe in the duality of good and evil. The epitome of evil, of which I believe is insanity, because there is a point which is incomprehensible to me. Not that I care to make much of it comprehensible to begin with.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yes, but by believing it to be a duality, you must also believe it to be one and the same. Whereas if one were to believe that good and evil are not a duality, that person must also believe it to be more of the same. <br /> <br /> <br /> However, I actually don't know what good and evil are, so I am probably wrong about my above description.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>How can I know if what I claim I know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I might not know? How can I know if what I claim I don't know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I do know?<br /> -Zhuangzi","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1715-ostarion","name":"Ostarion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1715_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"305"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-11T03:23:02-07:00","id":436870,"title":"","content":"I don't know personally. I felt practically born atheist but I admire and to be honest am deeply envious of believers because I really want to believe in God, in any God but I just can't. Question too much I guess, but I often feel hungry to talk about God and the nature of, sometimes feel like I straddle the lines in my mind of theist or atheist.<br /> <br /> But anyway, Good and Evil seem to be wholly social acts. Evil thoughts are not something society dislikes so much as it can effect your actions even in avoidance. The basics most agree with is being bad to others, but different societies can alleviate or worsen how bad it is. So one person says being evil is being gay because it causes XYZ. Nevermind it doesn't cause XYZ but now that society links it to a bad effect that harms other people, then it becomes a major concern. <br /> <br /> On the other hand, murder is portrayed universally as bad but people need wars for protection and/or self benefit. This is why throughout history, Wars are shown to be good and bad. They hurt a great many people, but many people fighting (on both sides) are protecting the interests of that nation, which is good. Nevermind that murder for religious purposes for a variety of reasons across nearly all cultures at one time or another is good while to non beleivers it is pure evil because someone is hurting for no reason.<br /> <br /> Good is helping others, benefiting others as how society depicts it but nearly universally it is helping someone, and usually the best is helping a great deal (saving someones life)or a great many(giving free coffee to everyone in a traffic jam) <br /> Evil is hurting others as a given society depicts it, universally it is hurting someone, and usually the worst is hurting a great deal (torture, killing)or a great many (being the guy who held up the traffic jam for a silly reason)<br /> <br /> But Killing for others is considered much less bad or not at all depending how the Others feel about the killing or who the Others are. Saving someone's life accidentally is considered fine and good, but luck did that, despite it would have been impossible if you weren't there. We get angry, hurt, and happy over these little acts. While society is better off in the world for all the people who do countless jobs for money, it becomes much less honored because it is also, a bit for themselves, thus selfish and less an altruistic act for lack of society benefiting wholly.<br /> <br /> No one is wholly bad because not every action is completely filled with malice of the most possible kind because no one has that energy. They would have to try to be Evil and really work at it and still fail. No one is wholly good because we are beings that have and care about personal needs that either coincide or become more important to us than altruism. Although I think this only applies to god, when we insist He/they must be good, which isn't always the default of religions nor does it need to be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5197-kadakithis","name":"kadakithis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-11T07:30:06-07:00","id":437040,"title":"","content":"It's never a good idea to dismiss an argument because the extreme cases in it aren't believable. (even though we can easily see extreme cases) In the duality of good-evil, how often do we fall back on Nazism as one of those extremes? Even today as we study that timeframe we find it unbelievable. And in such a study we can find the opposite. Regardless of motive, whether it's out of a sense of guilt, selfishness, whatever, we can find acts of extreme courage, kindness and decency. Such extremes of inhumanity and humanity doesn't dismiss the case of the good/evil ideal. It reinforces it.<br /> Many here live in a society where such extremes no longer exist, (or they aren't in contact with the wrong crowd...*sigh* I wish that were the case where I am concerned) Simply because many no longer see such extremes doesn't mean they can't exist. It just means that most aren't so blatantly &quot;tested.&quot;<br /> <br /> The good/evil concept exists independently of religion. We can see the evils done in the name of religion as easily as they were done promoting atheism.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T17:13:15-07:00","id":492105,"title":"","content":"True, but extending an argument to its logical extreme and then examining the result can be an informative exercise, as long as you don't make the mistake of dismissing the less extreme form because the extreme form is flawed. Let's take religion.<br /> <br /> Religion can and does act as a positive force in a great many peoples lives. It serves to illustrate morals in a form some people find easy to understand and accept, as well as providing structure and meaning to the inevitable crap life includes, as well as (often) an incentive to try to better yourself or society. A person who works for charities in order to live up to Paul's statement about charity being one of the primary ways of honouring God through honouring each other, is a perfect example of this. However, when we extend it to extreme forms, we end up with something most people would say is not cool - I'm not bringing in an example here because it seems like a bad idea, but you get the point. By examining the ways these same ideas, once thought positive and good, are damaging and harmful, we learn something about <i>why</i> these values can be good, and how humanity prioritises things. Which is very informative and useful.<br /> <br /> Yes, the above has flaws all over the place. I was going for more illustrative rather than strictly correct.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T21:46:32-07:00","id":492318,"title":"","content":"I may regret getting involved in this, but it seems to be a civil discussion, so here goes. :-)<br /> The problem with the &quot;good/evil&quot; argument is that so much of it is defined in the context of the society and culture that makes those definitions. At varying time through history, things like slavery, mass ritual murder, denial of [what we consider] basic rights to one group of people or another, public executions, and outright wars of genocide were considered &quot;the norm&quot; in their cultures. To them, these things weren't &quot;evil&quot;, they were just &quot;the way things are&quot;. 21st Century Western civilization takes a dim view on the majority these things, and might well deem them &quot;evil&quot; by our standards, but is that a true definition of &quot;good&quot; or &quot;evil&quot;? In a few decades/centuries/millennia, those definitions will be changed again, and whatever religions are prominent then will have new standards to apply to life. The God/Goddess that is most prominent will have a new set of rules, and people will use those rules to judge their lives and those of others around them. The notion that &quot;good/evil&quot; are anything to do with a divinity is simply not backed up by any evidence; it is nothing more than a set of arbitrary rules defined by churches, governments, and cultures.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T22:12:27-07:00","id":492339,"title":"","content":"Early civilizations were just that; young and growing. Early laws were invariably harsh with punishment...not so now. Growing, learning (in particular from mistakes made I should hope)...and maturing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T22:31:43-07:00","id":492347,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Purple Snit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=492318#post492318\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I may regret getting involved in this, but it seems to be a civil discussion, so here goes. :-)<br /> The problem with the &quot;good/evil&quot; argument is that so much of it is defined in the context of the society and culture that makes those definitions. At varying time through history, things like slavery, mass ritual murder, denial of [what we consider] basic rights to one group of people or another, public executions, and outright wars of genocide were considered &quot;the norm&quot; in their cultures. To them, these things weren't &quot;evil&quot;, they were just &quot;the way things are&quot;. 21st Century Western civilization takes a dim view on the majority these things, and might well deem them &quot;evil&quot; by our standards, but is that a true definition of &quot;good&quot; or &quot;evil&quot;? In a few decades/centuries/millennia, those definitions will be changed again, and whatever religions are prominent then will have new standards to apply to life. The God/Goddess that is most prominent will have a new set of rules, and people will use those rules to judge their lives and those of others around them. The notion that &quot;good/evil&quot; are anything to do with a divinity is simply not backed up by any evidence; it is nothing more than a set of arbitrary rules defined by churches, governments, and cultures.</div> </div> </div> </div>You had me somewhat until arbitrary popped up as a word. It's not really arbitrary. While a paragraph or two, let alone reams and lifetimes of learning, studies, books, various sciences from neuropsychology, genetics etc will all point to pieces of what makes up human morality it's not really something you can encapsule with short words. That said there is much at work and it's not arbitrary. Something I touched on I think in one of the other religion threads before someone tried to intimidate me out of the thread was how there are evolutionary angles to morality and why societies generally do not approve of genocide and other activities that are not conducive to the survival of a social animal like humans. To touch on something another person posted. I don't think of Good and Evil in those upper case, objective sense. It's a cartoonish sense of the human condition far far overblown in relation to the nature of the universe and our tiny speck of it. It's an ongoing process that will continue as long as the human race continues.<br /> <br /> And not to be mean to Prommie. But no. I haven't sinned, I haven't fallen short of a bloody thing. I'll leave that to the people that believe in that sort of thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T22:48:57-07:00","id":492364,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/306933-religion-thread?p=492347#post492347\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While a paragraph or two, let alone reams and lifetimes of learning, studies, books, various sciences from neuropsychology, genetics etc will all point to pieces of what makes up human morality it's not really something you can encapsule with short words.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree.<br /> You don't need words at all. We all invariably judge a person by his/her actions and how they relate to human society. I don't know when that has ever been different. When civilization was young, it made mistakes. Today, well, it's still making mistakes, but I believe that we are progressing. We don't follow the Code of Hammurabi anymore than we follow the Code of Urukagina. Slavery is gone from most countries today, etc...<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-26T11:18:31-07:00","id":492745,"title":"","content":"Must be Easter. Religion thread came back from the dead.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}